      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{58c6cbcffd04b1785f747b6c|full_name}: It does a GC anyways in the large menu which hitches all over the place already and they didn't need to run GC on quick menu open in 2017. I'm noting a regression which isn't great and would be nice to have fixed since I use my quick menu to check frame rate, emote, look at avatar performance and other stuff somewhat more frequently than I open the main avatar/world/social menu. And if it's not fixed, when someone says a few months from now \"but it has always been bad like this\" I can point at this issue and tell them it wasn't before a specific patch (this has happened with other bugs in the past). \n\n\nIMO they should leave GC to Unity for everything except loading screens since there is next to no determinism when memory will need to be allocated in VRC and manual GC is intended for when you know for a fact when things will need to be allocated for a scene and that they will not need to be freed for a deterministic lifetime. I've seen a few games that had VRC's situation where they were worried about large GC's taking a long time and tried the fix of \"but we'll just put a GC here and there and the hitches will be smaller.\" But the end result of that usually just ends with them putting more hitches in their game because GC has a flat overhead regardless of how much memory actually needs to be cleared. And it turns out that the algorithms made to work fast on billions of devices, with hundreds of thousands of different software packages, generally work better when you let them do their thing instead of going \"ah yeah let's put a GC in when you open the quick menu.\" Normally the answers developers land on are to let the GC do its thing, flatten reference chains, allocate less garbage, or attempt to optimize the GC process itself if they have the resources to do that. If you have an app that has a deterministic pattern to allocating and freeing memory, that's usually the rare case where you know better than the garbage collector. And time spent by VRC attempting to solve GC better than well established algorithms could be better spent on other things until they move to 2019.4 where incremental GC is a thing that mostly solves their issue assuming they aren't churning through enough garbage to stall the incremental GC. If the garbage collection is broken like the periodic hitch canny seemed to indicate last I heard of it, then it's a regression that Unity should be contacted about. Though since enabling logging UI triggers the periodic hitches in the open beta again I guess something in that was causing erroneous GC's that didn't happen in 2017? It would be nice to have the in game logging and info on who's master without sacrificing your performance to the 2018 gods though so I hope that's fixed soon as well. \n\n\nBut I digress, the issue is under review so hopefully it'll be fixed in the next open beta and I can't even know for certain if it's a GC causing this. ¯\\_(ツ)_/¯",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
